\\n      \\\"targetNode\\\": 120582,\\n      \\\"targetNodeName\\\": \\\"<Node element_id='4:262a5ae4-fd13-4dfb-9df2-60daf86edaff:120582' labels=frozenset({'Article'}) properties={'index': '5b4b8a03-db88-45dd-ba8b-1b57f6fb4f9e', 'abstract': 'Automatic program repair has been a longstanding goal in software engineering, yet debugging remains a largely manual process. We introduce a fully automated method for locating and repairing bugs in software. The approach works on off-the-shelf legacy applications and does not require formal specifications, program annotations or special coding practices. Once a program fault is discovered, an extended form of genetic programming is used to evolve program variants until one is found that both retains required functionality and also avoids the defect in question. Standard test cases are used to exercise the fault and to encode program requirements. After a successful repair has been discovered, it is minimized using structural differencing algorithms and delta debugging. We describe the proposed method and report experimental results demonstrating that it can successfully repair ten different C programs totaling 63,000 lines in under 200 seconds, on average.', 'title': 'Automatically finding patches using genetic programming', 'year': 2009, 'n_citation': 385}>\\\",\\n      \\\"totalCost\\\": 1.0,\\n      \\\"nodeIds\\\": [\\n        14034,\\n        120582\\n      ],\\n      \\\"nodeNames\\\": [\\n        \\\"<Node element_id='4:262a5ae4-fd13-4dfb-9df2-60daf86edaff:14034' labels=frozenset({'Article'}) properties={'index': '8ef983f4-b7d2-4d12-ae84-ac43d9a3ada7', 'abstract': 'Randomized algorithms have been used to successfully address many different types of software engineering problems. This type of algorithms employ a degree of randomness as part of their logic. Randomized algorithms are useful for difficult problems where a precise solution cannot be derived in a deterministic way within reasonable time. However, randomized a